Earlier this year, I participated for a few weeks in the newsgroup for
"The Sims [2]". On the one hand, the average cluedness of the participants
was lower and the contents of the messages were less useful than in rgrn.
Also, the messages were in general poorly formatted, and the relations
between real names and pseudonyms, and between users of newsservers and
Google Group posters were worse than in rgrn.

In general, I found that some secondary traits serve as a good indication
for what to expect from the quality of a newsgroup (not necessarily of
individual posters). I assume that the correlation is not incidental; form
and content are just two aspects of caring about the overall quality of a
message. Because rgrn posters care much about content, they also care
much about form.

